WEBVTT

1
00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:24.010
Andrzej Kopeć: Hello.

2
00:05:44.250 --> 00:05:45.020
Sergey Kisel: Hello?

3
00:05:45.910 --> 00:05:47.120
Sergey Kisel: Do you hear me?

4
00:05:48.640 --> 00:05:50.010
Andrzej Kopeć: Yes, I can hear you.

5
00:05:50.190 --> 00:05:54.140
Sergey Kisel: Okay, I was disconnecting, connecting!

6
00:06:01.600 --> 00:06:03.320
Sergey Kisel: Kevin, we don't hear you.

7
00:06:58.250 --> 00:06:59.509
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): Okay, do you hear me now?

8
00:07:01.320 --> 00:07:03.790
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): Oh, okay. Sorry, sorry about that.

9
00:07:10.610 --> 00:07:13.030
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): So I should be sharing my window now.

10
00:07:14.610 --> 00:07:28.950
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): the… this is… what I was walking you through silently was the new process for meeting agendas. Instead of keeping a running Google Doc, we'll keep them in Markdown documents in our repository.

11
00:07:29.210 --> 00:07:33.390
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): And so I… so I… I have that here on the screen.

12
00:07:33.480 --> 00:07:44.599
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): I will create a pull request for the meeting, or some meeting host will create a pull request in the repository. You can just directly modify the pull request to add agenda items.

13
00:07:44.650 --> 00:08:01.400
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): You can do it by… by committing to the pull request, you can send us a pull request, we can merge, you can do it live in the GitHub UI, like I was showing. The one… the one thing you need to do if you want to edit live in the GitHub UI is you need to make sure that you're on… on the branch, and not on just a commit hash.

14
00:08:03.800 --> 00:08:11.460
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): So that's the meeting agenda. We will abandon the old Google Doc that we had, the running, the running doc.

15
00:08:13.140 --> 00:08:24.059
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): I have a couple of announcements, so one is that we're branching for the next Compact release today, so end of day U.S. Central time.

16
00:08:24.340 --> 00:08:36.870
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): I will be the release manager for this release, so if you're… if you're one of the maintainers and you have changes that you want to get in, get them in by the end of the day, let me know if you can.

17
00:08:37.169 --> 00:08:40.610
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): And then we'll cut a pre-release and hand it to QA.

18
00:08:40.919 --> 00:08:47.569
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): And then I describe the new meeting agenda process, and it's basically right there.

19
00:08:48.520 --> 00:08:58.210
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): The other thing I wanted to, talk about was, the COIP process. This is the Compact Improvement Process. So I made a little slideshow.

20
00:08:58.590 --> 00:09:02.119
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): Can you see the slideshow on screen?

21
00:09:03.360 --> 00:09:07.459
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): Yes, okay. So this is the compact improvement process.

22
00:09:09.830 --> 00:09:22.469
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): What… what is it? A COIP is a compact improvement proposal. It's a formalized design document for the compact community. It's… it's a way of…

23
00:09:22.610 --> 00:09:34.640
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): it's a community-driven way to do open language design. So you'll write a… you'll write a proposal for the feature you want. It will go through a certain life cycle that I'll describe later.

24
00:09:35.050 --> 00:09:46.870
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): It is… the Minnikawa project intends this to be the primary mechanism for proposing new features, getting community input on features, and documenting the design decisions.

25
00:09:47.670 --> 00:09:55.209
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): So a sort of interesting thing is that… well, this is forked from the midnight improvement process, which has more or less exactly the same language.

26
00:09:55.930 --> 00:10:04.100
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): And the midnight improvement process was forked from the Cardano improvement process, which also has more or less exactly the same language.

27
00:10:04.730 --> 00:10:20.830
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): The Cardano improvement process may have been forked from the Ethereum improvement process, I am not sure of the history. There's very, very similar language in the Ethereum improvement process. I think they all ultimately came from the Python enhancement process, PAP.

28
00:10:21.310 --> 00:10:34.099
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): So, this language that is duplicated all over the place, I believe, actually originated with the PEP process. And so I'm going to talk about a lifestyle, a life cycle that we're proposing for COIP.

29
00:10:34.170 --> 00:10:44.759
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): Which is very similar to the way that the PEP works in practice. I have some experience working with the Python typing community as a PEP author and reviewer.

30
00:10:45.050 --> 00:10:52.520
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): Shepherding pips, and so, I know, I know some things that work well and some things that don't work well in that community.

31
00:10:53.610 --> 00:11:10.359
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): So, if we didn't like the name Koip, by the way, we could call it a CEP, Compact Enhancement Proposal, to just, give a hat tip back to the original PEP, or we could even call it a MEP, Minakawa Enhancement Proposal, but I don't suggest either of those.

32
00:11:11.780 --> 00:11:13.980
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): So, I'm proposing GOIP.

33
00:11:14.960 --> 00:11:20.000
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): What is… so the… the thing I want to talk about, mostly here is what is the life cycle of a COIP?

34
00:11:21.770 --> 00:11:23.310
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): Oh, go ahead, Thomas.

35
00:11:24.300 --> 00:11:29.290
Thomas Kerber: This is maybe a good time to ask, because I'm still confused about

36
00:11:29.740 --> 00:11:35.650
Thomas Kerber: the Minakawa name, and when we refer to things as Minakawa, and when as compact.

37
00:11:35.790 --> 00:11:38.620
Thomas Kerber: And it seems like we're… especially with Quote, because the…

38
00:11:39.570 --> 00:11:50.090
Thomas Kerber: these things are sticky, it is relevant, so I'm curious what you're thinking on what these two names represent right now.

39
00:11:50.490 --> 00:12:01.419
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): Yeah, so the naming… that's a good question, and I'll just say, anyone, raise your hand if you want to interrupt me during this presentation. Don't sit on your questions.

40
00:12:02.710 --> 00:12:19.659
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): it's a… it's a bit of a… it's a bit of a mess, and so the story is, the full disclosure, the Midnight Foundation told us that we could not use the name Compact for an open source project, because they wanted to… they wanted to keep the name for themselves, and so we had to come up with a different name.

41
00:12:19.820 --> 00:12:36.530
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): And when they saw in practice what it meant for us to be using a different name for the language, they realized that it was just not worth it to try to have two different names for the same thing. It caused all kinds of confusion, and so they said, actually, you can use the name Compact. So, the project is called Minakawa.

42
00:12:36.750 --> 00:12:42.330
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): The… that is the open source project that develops the compact language.

43
00:12:42.600 --> 00:12:44.440
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): The language is called Compact.

44
00:12:44.680 --> 00:12:56.569
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): The only thing that the Minakawa project does is develop the compact language. So it's a bit… so it is a bit of a mess. So Minakawa is the… is the org… open source organization.

45
00:12:58.870 --> 00:13:05.180
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): I sort of want to call this the compact improvement process, because you're proposing to improve Compact.

46
00:13:05.500 --> 00:13:10.069
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): You're not proposing to improve the open source organization.

47
00:13:10.850 --> 00:13:13.539
Thomas Kerber: Yeah, that makes sense. I think my… my…

48
00:13:14.030 --> 00:13:20.759
Thomas Kerber: past understanding was that we eventually wanted to go to naming everything Menakawa, but.

49
00:13:20.760 --> 00:13:30.030
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): We did. We did until the Midnight Foundation saw what that was going to look like in practice, and realized that it was not what they really wanted.

50
00:13:30.030 --> 00:13:35.709
Thomas Kerber: Okay, okay, I now appreciate where this mess comes from.

51
00:13:36.040 --> 00:13:36.710
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): Yes.

52
00:13:37.220 --> 00:13:42.789
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): Yeah, this was a mess that was imposed on us, and it's a little bit unfortunate, but that's where we are right now.

53
00:13:43.340 --> 00:14:00.719
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): So let's… so let's go to the COIP life cycle. So step one is that somebody has an idea, and this is anybody. This can be, anybody in the community, this can be any… any third party, and this can be the maintainers themselves, who have an idea for something that they want to improve.

54
00:14:00.790 --> 00:14:13.060
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): So, an important thing is that the maintainers will be following this process, too. There's no backdoor that the maintainers have to just do stuff that they want to do without getting community involvement, through this process.

55
00:14:14.260 --> 00:14:26.710
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): You… there might be a feature request in the repository, that… and I think that's a good idea… a good way to start such… such a, life cycle, but it doesn't have to be.

56
00:14:28.870 --> 00:14:46.080
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): it's a good idea to engage with the community before you start writing something, because you're saving yourself effort, wasted effort, by going down a wrong path, and it's a good idea to engage with the TSC, so eventually the TSC will vote to, to approve or reject the proposal.

57
00:14:46.140 --> 00:14:49.690
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): And it's a good idea to just have them on board early in the process.

58
00:14:50.040 --> 00:14:57.309
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): It's certainly legitimate to sound them out and say, you know, is this the way you guys want it, or is there something else that you'd like?

59
00:15:00.870 --> 00:15:05.749
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): Not every… so… so an important point is that not every improvement to Compact needs a COIP.

60
00:15:05.880 --> 00:15:11.709
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): Examples of things that don't need a coipe are, like, bug fixes. We won't… we don't need a proposal to fix a bug.

61
00:15:11.830 --> 00:15:15.980
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): Performance improvements, adding a flag to a tool.

62
00:15:16.130 --> 00:15:27.959
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): improving your error message, refactoring your implementations, like splitting files up into multiple files, or merging things, or reordering compiler passes. None of that requires a wipe. Kent, go ahead.

63
00:15:28.800 --> 00:15:31.260
Kent Dybvig: Of course, this is the back door you said we didn't have.

64
00:15:32.700 --> 00:15:36.220
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): Yeah, so there's a ju… there's a… there's… there's judgment there.

65
00:15:36.370 --> 00:15:50.320
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): that, and the way that this works in Python is somebody will have an idea, they'll say, hey, they'll file an issue, typically, and they'll say, could you change this? And one of the core developers, which is our maintainers, will say.

66
00:15:50.320 --> 00:15:52.070
Kent Dybvig: I think this needs APEP.

67
00:15:52.670 --> 00:15:55.270
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): for us to… for us to do this needs a PEP.

68
00:15:55.510 --> 00:16:03.879
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): It needs community involvement, it needs more input, it needs a more detailed design than just a ticket, an issue in the issue tracker.

69
00:16:04.170 --> 00:16:17.069
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): And often, other core developers will say, no, it doesn't. And there'll be a discussion when the core does, and eventually they'll come to some kind of consensus that they would like to see a proposal, or it's not necessary.

70
00:16:18.480 --> 00:16:28.599
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): The… the… so in this… in this case, it's the maintainers, and the… and the accountability is that if the community thinks that the maintainers are

71
00:16:28.790 --> 00:16:31.710
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): Dictatorial and refusing to accept

72
00:16:32.130 --> 00:16:40.529
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): to accept changes like this, then the community will go away. So the maintainers are presumably interested in growing a community.

73
00:16:41.010 --> 00:16:49.310
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): But yeah, you're absolutely right. There's a really fine line between having so much process that you can't get things done.

74
00:16:49.700 --> 00:16:53.400
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): And, not having enough process.

75
00:16:55.820 --> 00:17:05.630
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): Yeah, and so it's… yeah, so it's ultimately the maintainers who will decide if they want to have a proposal for this feature, or if they will implement it without a proposal.

76
00:17:07.240 --> 00:17:32.210
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): A rule of thumb that I always used in Python is if there were a third-party implementation, so for Python typing, for instance, there were multiple implementations of type checkers, was it something that… fixing a bug in one of the implementations doesn't need a PEP, but adding a feature that would be required to be implemented across the different third-party implementations would require a specification or a design

77
00:17:32.210 --> 00:17:32.840
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): doc.

78
00:17:32.870 --> 00:17:37.199
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): And so that's a little bit of a rule of thumb to decide if it does need a…

79
00:17:37.370 --> 00:17:38.920
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): Does need a proposal or not.

80
00:17:43.100 --> 00:17:49.189
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): So, step 2 is the first draft.

81
00:17:49.500 --> 00:17:58.240
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): So, the, The authors, people who want to… somebody who wants to write a proposal.

82
00:17:58.750 --> 00:18:16.429
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): will, draft a proposal, and this can be done in any way that's convenient for the authors. So, what's normally done in the Python world is that you have a shared Google Doc, and you invite authors to collaborate on it. Or you have a GitHub repository somewhere, and you invite people to send pull requests to it.

83
00:18:17.140 --> 00:18:36.240
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): It can be anywhere. The authors at this point are engaging in writing a first draft. When they feel like they have a draft that they're ready for the community to look at, they will send it as a pull request to the Minakawa slash compact repository.

84
00:18:37.520 --> 00:18:44.150
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): And what the pull request is specifically doing is adding a document to this COIPs subdirectory.

85
00:18:44.460 --> 00:18:50.489
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): That has the title, COIP with no number. I mean, those are literal X's, COIP XXXX.

86
00:18:50.810 --> 00:18:58.010
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): So that document won't exist, because we'll never actually merge a PR that has an unnumbered COIP.

87
00:18:58.900 --> 00:19:08.200
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): And you can create a subdirectory there that has any other resources that you need to, like embedded images or whatever, for your architectural diagrams.

88
00:19:09.370 --> 00:19:15.349
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): And, you make the PR not draft status. You tell the TSC somehow.

89
00:19:15.620 --> 00:19:26.240
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): I mean, pinging TSC members in the PR is typically how it's done, that it's… that it's ready for the TSC to consider it.

90
00:19:26.450 --> 00:19:31.569
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): So you feel like it's a first draft that… that fits the form of a quoip.

91
00:19:33.020 --> 00:19:37.049
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): And so, what the TSC does at this point is they consider the COIP.

92
00:19:37.050 --> 00:19:53.409
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): They will take it up at one of the next two scheduled meetings, so normally it'll be the next meeting, but if we're meeting on Wednesday and you send us something on Tuesday night or Wednesday morning, our time, you know, local time or whatever, we don't necessarily have a chance to take a look at it, and we'll say we'll look at it in a week.

93
00:19:54.350 --> 00:20:04.769
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): And we might get to this community call or whatever TSC meeting and say none of us have had a chance to actually look at it, so we're going to wait a week, but we'll have homework to do for next week.

94
00:20:05.920 --> 00:20:15.110
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): At this point, the consideration for the TSC is, does it… is it well-motivated? Does it solve a legitimate, genuine problem that people are having?

95
00:20:15.750 --> 00:20:22.169
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): Is it… is this proposed solution likely to be technically feasible, or is there something that makes it impossible?

96
00:20:22.380 --> 00:20:27.020
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): for us to implement it. Just, just not practical or not possible.

97
00:20:27.750 --> 00:20:35.009
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): Does it fit the structure of a COIP proposal? We have… there'll be a template, and you can just copy the template.

98
00:20:35.330 --> 00:20:52.779
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): Is it clearly written, grammatically correct? Is it understandable? Can we… so you would… you wouldn't be surprised, probably, but, like, in the Python community, you get… you get proposals that don't even make sense, like, you can't… and maybe in this world of AI-generated text, we'll get a lot of those.

99
00:20:52.830 --> 00:20:59.049
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): So you need to sort of disentangle it and see, does it… is it actually clear what's… what's being proposed here?

100
00:20:59.380 --> 00:21:15.659
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): But, in general, the TSC is pretty lenient at this point, because our preference here is to move ideas along for further discussion. We're not… we're not voting to approve at this point, we're voting just to accept that this is a thing that the community wants.

101
00:21:15.770 --> 00:21:17.930
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): That some people in the community want.

102
00:21:19.400 --> 00:21:31.440
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): And so the TSC will vote to accept, not approve, and sometimes I will mess up and write or say the wrong one of those two things, so maybe we should come up with a different terminology.

103
00:21:31.680 --> 00:21:44.450
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): And then the TSC assigns a number. So the COIP authors can't practically assign a number without clashing, and it's confusing if there's ever two different COIPs with the same number. So the TSC will assign the next available COIP number.

104
00:21:45.470 --> 00:22:00.290
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): Then the TSC will actually just take care of updating the PR to use the number in the text of the document, to rename the file, to rename the resources subdirectory if necessary, and anything else to use the correct number.

105
00:22:00.520 --> 00:22:07.340
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): We'll maintain a COIP index, and it'll be added as a draft COIP to the index.

106
00:22:07.760 --> 00:22:09.340
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): And then we'll merge that PR.

107
00:22:11.220 --> 00:22:19.310
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): I think we'll add the authors, if we… if this is feasible for us to do, we'll add the authors as co-owners. There's code owners for that document.

108
00:22:19.320 --> 00:22:32.009
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): So that they can, modify… they can send pull requests, and they can merge themselves without requiring the TSC to do it. So they can work… they can work as authors in public now, on that document.

109
00:22:32.570 --> 00:22:38.549
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): And we'll make sure that there's an improvement, issue filed that is in status draft.

110
00:22:38.700 --> 00:22:43.249
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): So that we can track the status of the Koip as it goes through its life cycle.

111
00:22:43.940 --> 00:22:54.289
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): And so the authors can now work on finishing the COIP. They can get public comment, they can get help from other authors, they can get, they can address comments.

112
00:22:54.450 --> 00:23:02.919
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): There's one… one thing that's sometimes done is a separate… a separate channel in some forum is created for people to discuss

113
00:23:03.040 --> 00:23:08.039
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): the… the issues. For very contentious things, this is necessary. For smaller things, it's not…

114
00:23:10.740 --> 00:23:28.279
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): And then the step 5 is when the authors are happy, they submit it. And the way they submit it is they'll just change the issue that's tracking the life cycle to be submitted. And at this point, they'll stop making substantial changes, so that we get a chance to actually see it in a sort of stable form.

115
00:23:29.030 --> 00:23:31.320
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): And there will be a period of public comment.

116
00:23:31.580 --> 00:23:36.269
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): I think we… I think we need to allow at least 2 weeks. Ideally, we…

117
00:23:36.660 --> 00:23:47.489
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): We have enough time that we can hear all the comments that people have, that people have time to engage with it, because people who are interested will not necessarily be able to drop everything to engage immediately with this.

118
00:23:49.460 --> 00:23:58.970
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): And authors can revise… if authors get comments, they should have been getting comments before, but if they're getting comments now, and they need to revise, they can do that.

119
00:23:59.610 --> 00:24:11.670
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): when… when comment is ended, and we feel… and the authors feel like the document is ready, the TSC will consider it at a meeting, and we will vote to accept or reject at that point.

120
00:24:13.950 --> 00:24:32.070
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): And then, if it gets approved, what happens? Well, if it gets approved, the… represents the TSC's agreement to implement the proposal on behalf of the community to approve the proposal for the community. It doesn't necessarily mean that there's going to be any maintainers who are available to immediately implement it.

121
00:24:32.230 --> 00:24:38.510
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): If it requires other implementation work, it might have to wait, or if it doesn't fit the…

122
00:24:38.630 --> 00:24:49.159
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): the priorities, it might be farther out on our roadmap. Specifically, if it has breaking language changes, it might have to wait until the next planned major version increment.

123
00:24:52.270 --> 00:25:10.960
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): It does mean that, in principle, the maintainers now couldn't turn contributors who wanted to implement this away on the grounds that we weren't interested in implementing this, because the TSC has accepted this. So we are interested in implementing this. We might… we just don't necessarily have bandwidth to do it as maintainers.

124
00:25:11.720 --> 00:25:14.379
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): And obviously it doesn't mean we'll accept.

125
00:25:14.710 --> 00:25:17.930
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): bad, poor quality implementations.

126
00:25:18.440 --> 00:25:37.970
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): Important point is that the COIP is not a living document. The Python community learned this by… they were… for a while, they were trying to update PEPs to proposals that had been accepted to reflect the current state of the world as things… as bug fixes happened, or as changes to features that happened.

127
00:25:38.530 --> 00:25:51.099
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): And this is a mess, because it's a lot of work, and it's inconsistently done, so you never actually know if the document has been updated or not. So, the COIP is once accepted, it's just

128
00:25:51.260 --> 00:26:04.890
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): done. We've accepted that proposal. If things change, they're reflected in our other documents, like the language reference, or developer docs, or whatever. We don't try to keep the COIPs up to date.

129
00:26:06.920 --> 00:26:10.909
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): And that's… that's it for the… for the presentation.

130
00:26:12.910 --> 00:26:14.990
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): Are there any questions about any of that?

131
00:26:15.290 --> 00:26:16.330
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): Yeah, Emil.

132
00:26:19.090 --> 00:26:23.330
⟣ €₥ℵ∪ℓ: Yeah, just a quick question. Is there a public…

133
00:26:23.430 --> 00:26:31.119
⟣ €₥ℵ∪ℓ: roadmap for Compact that a COIP author can reference, like, say, I have an idea.

134
00:26:31.280 --> 00:26:37.729
